---
title: skoro
---

Skoro is a probabilistic modeling library written in Rust. Its model-building API produces
descriptive intermediate representations, so a wide range of inference algorithms can run over the
same model.

You write a model the way you would write ordinary code over structs: add parameters and random
quantities, then combine them with operations. Behind the scenes each value is a node in a
Bayesian network. That network can be sampled, scored, differentiated to second order, and
compiled into a straight-line program that runs on a CPU or GPU through
[burn](https://burn.dev).

```rust
{../../../crates/skoro/src/lib.rs:30..37}
```

What sets it apart:

- **Second-order and manifold-aware.** Skoro ships its own autodiff engine, with forward
  values, vector–Jacobian products and Hessian–vector products for every operation. Rotations
  and poses (SO(3), SE(3)) are first-class quantities, updated through retractions.
- **Models that describe themselves.** A density term records *bindings*: which nodes are its
  residual, its location, its whitening and its Fisher-information root. Estimators read the
  structure they need back off the model instead of asking the user to supply it twice.
- **Two densities over one graph.** A model scores with $p$ and generates with $q$, so an
  importance weight $\log p - \log q$ is a first-class quantity.

